// ============================================================================
// Resolution · TYPE HIERARCHY  (extends / implements)
//
// type_parent — the direct supertype edge; the supertype *reference* is resolved
// through the normal type resolver (type_ref_resolves), so a client type extending
// a library type (class MyList extends ArrayList) links straight to the lib type.
// type_ancestor — transitive closure, used for inherited-method lookup and
// vulnerable-type inheritance.
//
// Client-side hierarchy (the sub type comes from java_type_reference). Library
// internal hierarchy (lib type extends lib type, e.g. ArrayList -> AbstractList)
// is added in Engine 2 over lib_type_reference when deeper chains are needed.
//
// java_type_reference (18): 1 context  2 typeRegistryLinkHash (the SUB type)
//   17 typeReferenceUniqueHash
// ============================================================================

// ── type_parent(SubHash, SuperHash) ─────────────────────────────────────────
// The reference at context SUPER_TYPE / IMPLEMENTS_INTERFACE is owned by the sub
// type (col 2); resolving that reference yields the super type (client or lib).
// Filtered to depth "0" (col 7) — the base super/interface type itself, not its
// type arguments. Without this, `extends ArrayList<String>` would make String (a
// depth-1 type-argument ref that shares the SUPER_TYPE context) a parent, and
// inherited-method dispatch would pull in the type argument's members.
type_parent(sub, super) :- java_type_reference(_, "SUPER_TYPE", sub, _, _, _, _, "0", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, ref),
    type_ref_resolves(_, ref, super).
type_parent(sub, super) :- java_type_reference(_, "IMPLEMENTS_INTERFACE", sub, _, _, _, _, "0", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, ref),
    type_ref_resolves(_, ref, super).

// ── type_ancestor(SubHash, AncestorHash) — transitive closure ───────────────
type_ancestor(sub, anc) :- type_parent(sub, anc).
type_ancestor(sub, anc) :- type_parent(sub, mid), type_ancestor(mid, anc).
